> Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.

Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\ Sure, here's a simple C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text. This function uses a basic conversion for a few common markdown features like headings, bold text, and links. For a more comprehensive conversion, you would need to handle more markdown features and possibly use a library like `markdowncpp`.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;

    // Convert headings (#)
    std::regex headingRegex("#+(.*)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, headingRegex, "<h1>$1</h1>");

    // Convert bold text (**)
    std::regex boldRegex("\\*\*(.*?)\\*\*");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, boldRegex, "<strong>$1</strong>");

    // Convert links ([link](http://example.com))
    std::regex linkRegex("\\[(.*?)\\]\\((.*?)\\)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, linkRegex, "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");

    // Convert unordered lists ( * item 1 * item 2 * )
    std::regex listRegex("\\* (.*)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, listRegex, "<ul><li>$1</li></ul>");

    // Convert ordered lists (1. item 1 * item 2 * )
    std::regex orderedListRegex("\\d+\\. (.*)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, orderedListRegex, "<ol><li>$1</li></ol>");

    return html;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to search for markdown patterns and replace them with their HTML equivalents. Note that this is a very basic implementation and may not handle all markdown features or edge cases. For a more robust solution, consider using a dedicated library for markdown to HTML conversion.

